261	/// Create a regex from the given pattern using this HIR's configuration.
262	fn pattern_to_regex(&self, pattern: &str) -> Result<Regex, Error> {
263	// The settings we explicitly set here are intentionally a subset
264	// of the settings we have. The key point here is that our HIR
265	// expression is computed with the settings in mind, such that setting
266	// them here could actually lead to unintended behavior. For example,
267	// consider the pattern `(?U)a+`. This will get folded into the HIR
268	// as a non-greedy repetition operator which will in turn get printed
269	// to the concrete syntax as `a+?`, which is correct. But if we
270	// set the `swap_greed` option again, then we'll wind up with `(?U)a+?`
271	// which is equal to `a+` which is not the same as what we were given.
272	//
273	// We also don't need to apply `case_insensitive` since this gets
274	// folded into the HIR and would just cause us to do redundant work.
275	//
276	// Finally, we don't need to set `ignore_whitespace` since the concrete
277	// syntax emitted by the HIR printer never needs it.
278	//
279	// We set the rest of the options. Some of them are important, such as
280	// the size limit, and some of them are necessary to preserve the
281	// intention of the original pattern. For example, the Unicode flag
282	// will impact how the WordMatcher functions, namely, whether its
